  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "kB/gv8qoEvXLx57u/Sjjkp9XnDI=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:30:29.584Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "NextWave Consulting's priority is derived, not scored. The row was carried over from the v2 ranking and rescored mechanically on two inputs only \u2014 size and slot occupancy \u2014 and the record says so explicitly, together with the instruction to run the OSS-portfolio screen before acting. Its priority 2 therefore means the thesis question is open, not that the lead has been qualified.\n\nThe gap matters because the criteria that actually decide the interesting cases are the ones missing. Size and slot occupancy are mechanical: headcount and which platform is carried. Open-source posture, sovereignty alignment and whether the firm's portfolio suggests it would carry client-owned infrastructure all require reading the firm, and none of that has been done here. What is on file is an Appian practice \u2014 including an Appian EMEA Innovation Award \u2014 and v2 verticals of banking and risk.\n\nRecorded as a data-quality note rather than an assessment, this is a reminder that the workbook mixes two kinds of row: judged and rescored. The rescored ones look identical in the priority column and carry much less evidence, so any batch action taken on priority alone will treat them as equivalent when they are not.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**2 \u00b7 decide thesis first**\n- NextWave Consulting (UK)\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> DERIVED, NOT SCORED ON v3 \u2014 v2 row rescored mechanically (size + slot-occupancy only). Run the OSS-portfolio screen before acting. v2 verticals: Banking, risk",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:30:29.583999Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:30:29.584004Z"
    }
  },
